⟦200⟧may be treated very easily if the motions are all in the line joining source and receiver. Let <math>\text{S}</math> (fig. 20) be the source at a given instant, and let its frequency of vibration, or the number of waves it sends out per second, be <math>n</math>. Let <math>\text{S}'</math> be its position one second later, its velocity being <math>u</math>. Let <math>\text{R}</math> be the receiver at a given instant, <math>\text{R}'</math> its position a second later, its velocity being <math>v</math>. Let the velocity of the air from <math>\text{S}</math> to <math>\text{R}</math> be <math>w</math>, and let <math>\text{U}</math> be the velocity of sound in still air.
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{sc|Fig.}} 20.}}
If all were still, the <math>n</math> waves emitted by <math>\text{S}</math> in one second would spread over a length <math>\text{U}</math>. But through the wind velocity the first wave is carried to a distance <math>\text{U} + w</math> from <math>\text{S}</math>, while through the motion of the source the last wave is a distance <math>u</math> from <math>\text{S}</math>. Then the <math>n</math> waves occupy a space <math>\text{U} + w - u</math>. Now turning to the receiver, let us consider what length is occupied by the waves which pass him in one second. If he were at rest, it would be the waves in length <math>\text{U} + w</math>, for the wave passing him at the beginning of a second would be so far distant at the end of the second. But through his motion <math>v</math> in the second, he receives only the waves in distance <math>\text{U} + w - v</math>. Since there are <math>n</math> waves in distance <math>\text{U} + w - u</math> the number he actually receives is <math>n(\text{U} + w - v)/(\text{U} + w - u)</math>. If the velocities of source and receiver are equal then the frequency is not affected by their motion or by the wind. But if their velocities are different, the frequency of the waves received is affected both by these velocities and by that of the wind.

⟦1100⟧The change in pitch through motion of the source may be illustrated by putting a pitch-pipe in one end of a few feet of rubber tubing and blowing through the other end while the tubing is whirled round the head. An observer in the plane of the motion can easily hear a change in the pitch as the pitch-pipe moves to and from him.

⟦1300⟧''Musical Quality or Timbre.''—Though a musical note has definite pitch or frequency, notes of the same pitch emitted by different instruments have quite different quality or timbre. The three characteristics of a longitudinal periodic disturbance are its amplitude, the length after which it repeats itself, and its form, which may be represented by the shape of the displacement curve. Now the amplitude evidently corresponds to the loudness, and the length of period corresponds to the pitch or frequency. Hence we must put down the quality or timbre as depending on the form.

⟦1500⟧The simplest form of wave, so far as our sensation goes—that is, the one giving rise to a pure tone—is, we have every reason to suppose, one in which the displacement is represented by a harmonic curve or a curve of sines, <math>y=a \sin m(x-e)</math>. If we put this in the form <math>y=a \sin \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x-e)</math>, we see that <math>y=0</math>, for <math>x=e</math>, <math>e+\tfrac{1}{2}\lambda</math>, <math>e+\tfrac{2}{2}\lambda</math>, <math>e+\tfrac{3}{2}\lambda</math>, and so on, that <math>y</math> is <math>+</math> from <math>x=e</math> to <math>x=e+\tfrac{1}{2}\lambda</math>, <math>-</math>from <math>e+\tfrac{1}{2}\lambda</math> to <math>e+\tfrac{2}{2}\lambda</math>, and so on, and that it alternates between the values <math>+a</math> and <math>-a</math>.

⟦1800⟧The form of the curve is evidently as represented in fig. 21, and it may easily be drawn to exact scale from a table of sines.
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{sc|Fig.}} 21.}}

⟦2200⟧In this curve <math>\text{ABCD}</math> are nodes. <math>\text{OA}=e</math> is termed the epoch, being the distance from <math>\text{O}</math> of the first ascending node. <math>\text{AC}</math> is the shortest distance after which the curve begins to repeat itself; this length <math>\lambda</math> is termed the wave-length. The maximum height of the curve <math>\text{HM}=a</math> is the amplitude. If we transfer <math>\text{O}</math> to <math>\text{A}</math>, <math>e=0</math>, and the curve may be represented by <math>y=a \sin \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}x</math>.

⟦2400⟧If now the curve moves along unchanged in form in the direction <math>\text{ABC}</math> with uniform velocity <math>\text{U}</math>, the epoch <math>e=\text{OA}</math> at any time <math>t</math> will be <math>\text{U}t</math>, so that the value of <math>y</math> may be represented as
{{block center|<math>y=a \sin \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x-\text{U}t).</math> (16)}}

⟦2600⟧The velocity perpendicular to the axis of any point on the curve at a fixed distance <math>x</math> from <math>\text{O}</math> is
{{block center|<math>\frac{dy}{dt}=-\frac{2\pi\text{U}a}{\lambda}\cos\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x-\text{U}t).</math> (17)}}

⟦2800⟧The acceleration perpendicular to the axis is
{{block center|<math>\frac{d^2y}{dt^2}=-\frac{4\pi^2\text{U}^2a}{\lambda^2}\sin\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x-\text{U}t)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>=-\frac{4\pi^2\text{U}^2}{\lambda^2}y</math> (18)}}
which is an equation characteristic of simple harmonic motion.

⟦3000⟧The maximum velocity of a particle in the wave-train is the amplitude of <math>dy/dt</math>. It is, therefore,
{{block center|<math>u_m=2\pi\text{U}a/\lambda=2\pi na.</math> (19)}}
{{EB1911 sheet signature|XXV. 15}}

